> **4.7 THE BANNER — a member's riders under the crown** (RULINGS-ROUND3 §F; THE PATROLS own the riders' behaviour and write the section THE BANNER (PR11, §10.1 (c), §10.3), THE PASSAGE the crossing and its jurisdiction read (PS §14); PL owns the reads and the rows, written here). **The owner's words (2026-10-18 ~19:2x), verbatim:** "and i keep forgetting an important point, how do patrols work in a kingdom. I think patrols have multiple loyalties that allow flexibility but also closes a gap on things like civial war and such. Effectively, every patrol in a kingdom is working under the highest overlord banner, and every subsequent level until it reaches them inteh vassal chain with a home local font thet is their home settlement that they more or less take charge in and can with some flexibility operate in unity to cover the entirety of the kingdom region. This si also how we can express protection from the crown. so it blurs the line a bit between territories. What are your thoughts?" [CURED 2026-10-18 (fp-final r4) — RULINGS-ROUND4 A.7 · SEAMS-r3-6 (PL's half: "F.2's one share law and F.4's protection deed (the fealty record's rows)") · RULINGS-ROUND3 §F's PL side: F.1 cited, F.2 the row, F.3 the hold read, F.4 the rows, F.5 the break's effect on the stack, F.6 the grip and resentment counterforces.]
> - **F.1 One home, a stack of banners.** A patrol has ONE home — the settlement that raises, pays and houses it, with first call on its own roads (THE PATROLS' envelope is the home's). Its banners are the home's chain as the walk reads it — §2's BANNER STACK, `polityOf(…, homeId).path` with `crownReachAt` at each rung, never stored — so when the chain changes, the banners change at the next read. A lord's call on riders reaches them only through the one summons, rung by rung, never skipping a lord: the riders are an input line on `call_in_answer` (§4.1, OD-PL-50).
> - **F.2 One share law.** The liege's share of a member's riders IS the share of its voice in every decision: `crownReachAt(worldState, snapshot, rootId, memberId, rootId).share01` — `POLITY_LIEGE_SHARE[band]` for a direct vassal, `× POLITY_PARAMOUNT_REACH[band of the hold]` for a sub-vassal (PL4, PL5) — never a second share for riders. The crown's NET over the kingdom is THE PATROLS' one coverage composition over PL's reads: Σ over members m of riders(m) × `share01`(m) × `hold01`, the hold the crown's own read (§3.1 (e)), the per-stretch cap binding. PL owns the share and the hold; PR the composition and the cap.
> - **F.3 The line blurs by the hold.** STRONG — the crown's own read `staunch` or `unbroken` (§3.1 (e)): the kingdom is one net, and the crown composes its members' riders across their borders. Each RECEIVING member decides at its own border, on its own belief of the hold (§3.1 (f)), whether a fellow member's riders ride its roads BY RIGHT OF THE BANNER (at `staunch`+: by right, no tolerance spent) or cross on THE PASSAGE's ladder as allies with the ally size tolerance (below). WEAK — the crown's own read `frayed` or `shaky`: each lord keeps his own roads, the crown composes no net, and its own riders keep to the root's land (§15.12). No new stance word: PS's jurisdiction read cites §3.1 (f).
> - **F.4 The crown's protection is felt, and recorded as a deed.** THE OCCASION: a member answers its liege's summons `answered` WITH ITS RIDERS (their line sent, §4.1) while the crown's own read is `staunch`+ — the member's roads are now the net's to cover. Below `staunch` no occasion opens: each lord keeps his own roads, a crown that cannot hold the kingdom as one net promises no cover, and a weak crown's `P` is not drained by a cover it never offered. THE OUTCOME, ONE row on the member's edge to the liege whose summons it answered, episode the call-in request's key (the service row's own; PL's own row, the OBLIGATION family): `protection_kept` when riders of the banner — any rider not the member's own, the crown's or a fellow member's — were PLACED (PR4's weekly placement) on at least one of the member's road halves within the first season after the answer (`INTERVAL_WEEKS.one_season`, DRAFT, UNMEASURED); `protection_failed` when none were. Written once, at that season's end or when the member's riders come home, whichever is first; no patch (§4.9). "Came to its aid" is never a number: the PLACEMENT is the deed and the count of riders never enters (PL-V5's principle). A body sees one banner — what it believes of the kingdom's riders deters it by what it has heard (THE PATROLS' believed watch; THE BODIES cite); PL writes nothing for it. Lit only where THE PATROLS are (`patrolsEnabled`) and PL-5's summons exists (WC-5): before then no riders are summoned (OD-PL-50) and no occasion opens.
> - **F.5 Civil war, feud and break — the gap closed.** A BREAK re-points the breaker's link to `rival` (§5.5), so the walk no longer reaches its old liege: its patrols follow their home, and the crown's riders in its land are a FOREIGN UNIT — THE PASSAGE's rules apply to them as to any army, trespass by hostility included. A FEUD leaves every link sworn: the two members' patrols lose recognition by their pair's own war state (PR's friction), the stack above them unchanged. A POLITY WAR keeps every link sworn in storage, but while `swornLinkSuspended` holds, THE BANNER STACK is CUT at the suspended link (§2): a patrol follows its home's side, and the crown's riders in a claimant-side member's land are a foreign unit. Every banner reader — THE PATROLS' coverage composition and THE PASSAGE's jurisdiction read — reads the stack THROUGH the predicate and joins §5.3's GUARDED set (the walker reds a reader of the vassal type that does not). FIRST-VISIBLE (requirement 16): a patrol turned back at a border it rode freely the season before (THE PATROLS' and THE PASSAGE's line; PL-6's and PL-9's first sign wherever patrols ride).
> - **F.6 Counterforces (PL's half).** (a) THE LENDER'S CREDIT: a member whose riders the net placed on a fellow member's road halves in a year writes ONE `service_answered` row that year on its edge to its liege (episode `riders_lent:<rootId>:<yearStartTick>`, `yearStartTick = tick − (tick mod INTERVAL_WEEKS.one_year)`; PL's own row, the OBLIGATION family, no patch) — a credit the fealty question, the allegiance choice and the summons fork read. (b) THE SWEPT LORD'S GRUDGE: the crown's riders on a member's roads unasked wear as `riders_quartered` (§15.9). (c) THE PEACETIME CALL: where the crown composes a member's riders onto another's roads in a season in which neither the root nor the lender is at war, the lend wears as `riders_lent`, ONE typed decaying incident per lender per season on its sworn edge (§15.9's closed list, registered in PL's own commit): resentment is a grip term, so deference falls along the chain the crown sweeps ("a crown that calls riders from every member in peace pays in resentment across the chain"); in war the lend is service and wears nothing. (d) The per-stretch cap stops stacking, and feuding members share no eyes (THE PATROLS').
> - **F.7 The surface.** "The crown's riders keep the Tolle hills since Kell's riders went to the war." (the deed kept) · "No rider of the crown has come to the Tolle hills since Kell's riders went to the war." (failed) — §13. On scrutiny the patrol's card shows its home and its stack as the chain; the map draws the banner when the host lights patrols (the window, H9/H23 — never H16). "Banner" stays a PROSE word: PL mints no code token of that name (`banner` is landed 193 times in `src`, the UI word; `cure/D-PL-r4-probe/measure.out`).
