> **4.8 KEEPING THE PEACE — a body in a member's land** (THE BODIES `docs/DESIGN_FP_ARCH_BD.md` §7.1 and §10.2; their three routes to PL, `cure/L-BD.md` §4). **The owner's words (~19:1x), verbatim, the part that reaches the crown:** "settlemetns need to keep their own peace or be held responsible." Keeping the peace in one's own land is part of the member's SERVICE (PL8's "up"); the crown's part is its word and, under a firm hold, its ORDER to hunt; the price of defying that order is harbouring. Three pieces, one door:
> - **(1) `peace_unkept` — the member's default.** When a body based or born in a sworn member's land spills into a fellow member's or the liege's land (THE BODIES' spill, BD §10.2 (b), on BD's own clock and dwell — at most once per body per edge per dwell), THE BODIES REQUEST a `peace_unkept` row on the (liege ↔ member) edge through `requestFealtyRow`, episode `body:<kind>:<hash>:<tick>` (§4.6). It is the REQUESTED family's (§8): written under the requester's own flag ∧ `polityEnabled` — BD's `bodySpillEnabled`, whose liege face also needs `polityObligationsEnabled` (BD §13), so with obligations dark no row is requested. It carries NO PATCH (a default is a record, as `service_refused` is; §4.9): the pair's resentment moves once, by BD's peer face where the spill reaches the liege's own land (BD §10.2 (a)), never twice for one spill. Its readers are the fealty question, the allegiance choice and the summons fork (as every row on the edge) and the hunt order below; the hold's `P` never reads it (`P` is the liege's protection; this is the member's service). A body of any kind may be its subject — one cause family, the kind in the episode (THE BODIES' item 23b); a conclave's export is BD's to sign by faith (a conclave of the crown's own faith is no default) and reaches PL only as this one row when BD requests it. It is distinct from THE SITES' `den_unanswered` (the LIEGE's failure to clear a den pressing its member, a protection outcome): both may stand for one gang, one each way.
> - **(2) THE HUNT ORDER — the liege's word.** In EVERY band the liege's own word toward a body (BD's `COURT_STANCES`, its derived default or its heard word) is one input of its member's `body_stance` fork at `POLITY_LIEGE_SHARE[band]` (PL4; §3.2's `justice_at_home` row). Under `staunch`+ by its own read (§3.1 (d), the arbitration's idiom) the liege may ORDER the hunt: `huntOrderOf(worldState, snapshot, { liegeId, memberId, bodyId })` → `{ ordered, reasons }`, pure and draw-free, `ordered` when the liege's own word toward the body is `hunted` (for a gang; toward a beast it is always `hunt`, BD10) AND an occasion stands — a `peace_unkept` row for that body on the edge within its dwell, or that body's raid on the liege's own land within BD's dwell — or the DM directs it (his line on the card). The order is ONE INPUT LINE on the member's `body_stance` card (BD's kind; PL mints no kind — one fact, one hearing, the relief precedent); its weight in the fork is the same band share (one share law); what the order adds is the PRICE of refusing it. It is lit under `polityObligationsEnabled` (the crown's peace, PL-7) with THE BODIES' `bodiesEnabled`. A paramount's order reaches a sub-vassal through its mid-lord's own `body_stance` (whose fork reads the paramount's order as its liege's), rung by rung, never skipping a lord. The crown's OWN riders sweeping a member's roads are THE PATROLS' `PATROL_ORDER` by the crown (PR's instrument), worn as `riders_quartered` (§15.9).
> - **(3) HARBOURING A BODY — the price, never a break.** A member whose court keeps a gang `tolerated` (THE BODIES' derived `harboured`) while an ORDER of its liege to hunt that gang stands is harbouring the crown's enemy: THE BODIES request ONE `fealty_defied` row with its deference patch on the (liege ↔ member) edge through the door, episode `body:<kind>:<hash>:<orderTick>` (one per order), the REQUESTED family; it never opens `fealty_break` (PL7: the detector never reads the record). A beast is never harboured (item 23b: a court cannot harbour a pack — its neglect is `peace_unkept`); a conclave has no `harboured` word.
